I added a couple printk statements and some logic to build a stronger
intuition of how xv6's scheduling subsystem works.
You can run these commands to review the differences with the 
upstream MIT xv6 repo:

git clone https://github.com/abouzaiditiali/xv6-trace
cd xv6-trace
git remote add upstream https://github.com/mit-pdos/xv6-riscv.git
git fetch upstream
git diff upstream/riscv..sched-trace

You can then boot xv6 and see what happens. 
The tracing follows the format below:

[X | Y] [Z] fields

Here is an example:

[pid 2 | cpu 0] [SLEEP] chan=0x80019720

Y is the cpu that statement ran on.
X indicates the execution context on Y when that statement ran. 
The execution context can either be that of a process or that of the 
scheduler specific to that cpu.
Z is the event 'near' the printk statement. 
One thing to note is that all the printk statements I inserted are in 
uninterruptible sections so the cpu and execution context accurately
describe where the event takes place.

The example can be read as: pid 2 goes to sleep on channel 0x80019720. 
More precisely, the statement indicates that pid 2's state
changed from RUNNING to SLEEPING. That state transition happened on cpu 0
under pid 2's kernel execution context.

A useful technique is to 'git grep' the event [Z].
That way, you can see where the printk statement is. 

process lifecycle:
[WAKE]
[SWTCH]
[SLEEP]

asynchronous traps:
[VIRTIO0_IRQ]
[TIMER_IRQ]

synchronous traps:
[SYS]

and more.

Feel free to contribute in any way. 
If you read through here, here is a trace to meditate on.
It starts with init forking and ends with the shell printing the $ prompt.

I manually added an extra line somewhere. That line can never printed there 
in a normal trace. Try to find it...

